ma in a continent cutaneous pouch diversion. This points to a management pattern where the stoma may not be continuously draining (consistent with the broader description of stomas sometimes serving as access sites for catheter drainage rather than continuous output). [Data: Entities (5909, 2797)]\\n\\nThe relationship explicitly connects STOMA to BANDAGE, stating that the stoma can be covered by a small bandage in a continent cutaneous pouch setup. This adds a care/use-mode dimension (coverage and discretion) distinct from continuously collecting output in a bag. [Data: Relationships (13433); Entities (2797, 5909)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Device attachment and sealing are represented by an ostomy pouch system that uses an adhesive wafer\",\n      \"explanation\": \"An ostomy bag (ostomy pouch) is described as a disposable external bag attached to the abdomen that collects urine from a stoma in an ileal conduit and is held in place by an adhesive wafer. While this is a urinary context rather than stool, it shares the same stoma-based external collection concept and expands the community from fecal diversion into urologic diversion appliances. [Data: Entities (4895, 2797)]\\n\\nThe relationship between OSTOMY BAG (OSTOMY POUCH) and WAFER states that the wafer adheres to skin to help keep the ostomy bag attached and provides a watertight barrier, highlighting the technical role of adhesion and sealing in appliance performance and skin interface management. [Data: Relationships (10799); Entities (4895)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 3.0,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"Impact is low-to-moderate because the community describes clinically significant surgical diversion concepts and device systems, but provides no evidence of illicit activity, harmful intent, or reputational/legal risk beyond medical complexity. [Data: Entities (2797, 6193, 2798, 4898, 4895); Relationships (9431, 14217, 13423, 10798, 10799)]\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "fun